## Root cause
`objToLocalTS()` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` (the only file I changed).

JS `Date.UTC(y, ...)` maps years 0–99 to 19XX. The workaround re-applied **only the year**:

```js
d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
```

`Date.UTC` had already normalized the month/day overflow using the 19XX year's calendar. When the day overflow rolls the date into the *next* year (year 99 → year 100), that normalization used year 1999/2000 — and **2000 is a leap year while 100 is not**. So any date that passes through February after crossing the barrier came out one day early, e.g. `DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({ days: 62 })` returned `0100-03-02` instead of `0100-03-03` (and `0099-02-03 + 400d` → `0100-03-09` instead of `0100-03-10`). Cases that don't overflow past Dec 31 (like the existing `0099-12-31 + 2d` test) were unaffected, which is why the existing "100 barrier" test passed.

## Exact diff (src/impl/util.js, objToLocalTS)
```diff
   // for legacy reasons, years between 0 and 99 are interpreted as 19XX; revert that
   if (obj.year < 100 && obj.year >= 0) {
     d = new Date(d);
-    d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
+    // set the month and day again, this is necessary because year 2000 is a leap year, but year 100 is not
+    // so if obj.year is in 99, but obj.day makes it roll over into year 100,
+    // the calculations done by Date.UTC are using year 2000 - which is incorrect
+    d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day);
   }
```
Setting year+month+day together forces re-normalization in the *target* year's calendar (this matches upstream Luxon's fix).

